<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrew I of Hungary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1015–<a href="/years/1060">1060</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrew I was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from 1046 to 1060. A member of the Árpád dynasty who had lived in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, he returned during the pagan uprising of 1046 that overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-orseolo">Peter Orseolo</a>, and he restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. He resisted Emperor Henry III, whose campaigns of 1051 and 1052, including the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a>, failed, but he was overthrown by his brother Béla I and died of his wounds in 1060.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andrew-i-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Andrew I of Hungary" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: János Thuróczy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrew_I_(Chronica_Hungarorum).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Andrew belonged to a younger branch of the Árpád dynasty. Medieval sources give contradictory accounts of his parents, but modern historians agree that he and his brothers Levente and Béla were sons of Vazul and a concubine from the Tátony clan. According to the chronicles, King Stephen I had Vazul blinded in 1031, after the death of his own son Emeric, and the sons left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. The contemporary Annals of Altaich says that Stephen did this to secure the succession of his sister's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-orseolo">Peter Orseolo</a>.</p><p>The brothers went first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, where Béla married a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-ii-lambert">Mieszko II</a>. Andrew and Levente moved on, and in the late 1030s they reached the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, whose daughter Anastasia Andrew married.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-orseolo">Peter Orseolo</a> had alienated many lords and prelates, above all by recognising the suzerainty of Emperor Henry III in 1045. Discontented lords sent envoys to Andrew and Levente, but by the time they returned a revolt dominated by pagans had broken out. According to the Illuminated Chronicle the brothers gave in to the rebels' demands, and the pagans killed several prelates, among them Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerard-of-csanad">Gerard of Csanád</a>. Peter fled but was tricked into returning, captured and blinded.</p><p>Three surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> crowned Andrew at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szekesfehervar">Székesfehérvár</a> in late 1046 or in 1047. He soon broke with his pagan supporters, restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and declared pagan rites illegal, and his epithets the White and the Catholic are connected with these events.</p><p>Henry III invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in August 1051, but Andrew and Béla used scorched earth tactics and forced the imperial troops to withdraw. The emperor returned the next summer and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a>, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-ix">Pope Leo IX</a> mediated a peace he lifted the siege. Andrew had granted Béla a third of the kingdom as a duchy in 1048.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Andrew's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> was born in 1053, and Andrew tried to secure his succession against Béla, who had a strong claim by the principle of agnatic seniority. Struck by a stroke, he had the four-year-old Solomon crowned from the autumn of 1057. Béla fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and returned with Polish troops, while Bavarian, Bohemian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> troops came to Andrew's aid. Andrew lost the decisive battle east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a>, was badly wounded and died before Béla was crowned on 6 December 1060. He was buried in the crypt of Tihany Abbey, which he had founded in 1055.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andrew I of Hungary” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Agnes of Poitou, regent of the Holy Roman Empire for her son Henry IV, met King <b>Andrew I of Hungary</b> to negotiate the border in the area of Burgenland.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-20">20 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1048 King <b>Andrew I</b> granted a third of the Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) to his brother Béla, as a ducatus, or duchy.</blockquote><a href="/state/tercia-pars-regni">Tercia pars regni</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Zirc, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.26,17.87&amp;year=1044" aria-label="Zirc on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Andrew I of Hungary"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4301 6016h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4301 6016h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=47.26,17.87&amp;year=1044">What was here in 1044? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
